<title>ARGENTINA: Argentine debt stood at $97.1 bln by end-1996.</title>
<headline>Argentine debt stood at $97.1 bln by end-1996.</headline>
<dateline>BUENOS AIRES 1997-03-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>Argentine total public debt by end-1996 stood at $97.1 billion compared to $87.1 billion a year earlier, the Economy Ministry said Monday.</p>
<p>The ministry said there were $15.64 billion of public debt issues in the year, against $6.5 billion debt amortizations, with debt growing $9.14 billion, plus $1.17 billion of capitalization on interest on government bonds.  </p>
<p>The Economy Ministry gave the following breakdown of Argentine public sector debt by type of creditor:</p>
<p> Total bilateral debt			$10.16 billion</p>
<p>consisting of:</p>
<p>    - Paris Club				 6.73 billion</p>
<p>    - Other bilateral			  3.44 billion</p>
<p> Total multilateral debt		    16.37 billion</p>
<p>consisting of:</p>
<p>    - Inter-American Development Bank    4.76 billion</p>
<p>- International Bank for</p>
<p>	Reconstruction and Development     5.32 billion</p>
<p>    - International Monetary Fund	  6.28 billion</p>
<p>    - others				     0.01 billion</p>
<p> State suppliers				 0.28 billion</p>
<p> Private banks				   1.45 billion</p>
<p> Bonds and public debt paper		68.84 billion</p>
<p>    - in Argentine pesos		     8.17 billino</p>
<p>    - in foreign currency		   60.67 billion</p>
<p> TOTAL PUBLIC SECTOR DEBT		    97.10</p>
<p>(( -- Buenos Aires Newsroom +541 318-0695))</p>
